---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site.config';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'ProfilePage',
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    '@id': `${SITE.url}/#person`,
    name: SITE.fullName,
    alternateName: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.url,
    sameAs: [SITE.linkedin],
    worksFor: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Cloud Capital' },
    knowsAbout: ['AI economics', 'AI governance', 'Corporate governance', 'Carbon emissions', 'Startups', 'Venture investment'],
  },
};
---
<Base title="About" description="Tharshan Thavaharan builds in AI at Cloud Capital, founded AI Launchpad, and has published doctoral research in Finance Research Letters." type="profile" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <header class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="mono kicker"><span class="a">04</span> / about</p>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">I work on how AI is built, financed and governed, and on the people and institutions that decide how it turns out.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="wrap with-side about-body">
    <p class="meta stack"><span>tharshan thavaharan</span></p>
    <div>
    <div class="prose numbered">
      <h2>What I do</h2>
      <p>
        I’m at <strong>Cloud Capital</strong>, where I work with CFOs, CTOs and investors to underwrite AI and cloud infrastructure spend.
        Much of that comes down to the economics underneath AI: what it actually costs to run, who is accountable for that cost, and how it should be financed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before that, at Paddle, I founded <strong>AI Launchpad</strong>, an accelerator for AI founders. Across its cohorts it accelerated more than
        350 startups from over 90 countries, which is a useful way to see a technology wave from the inside, one early-stage company at a time.
        I’ve also built and exited a bootstrapped AI platform with friends.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alongside this, I advise builders, investors and governments on policy and investment decisions in human-wrapped AI.
      </p>

      <h2>Research</h2>
      <p>
        My doctoral research at Paris School of Business studies how corporate governance shapes carbon outcomes. Its main empirical work, co-authored
        with Hatem Rjiba, was <a href="/research/female-representation-boards-carbon-emissions">published in <em>Finance Research Letters</em></a> in 2022.
        Across 1,514 firms in 43 countries, companies with a higher share of women on their boards had lower carbon emission intensity.
      </p>
      <p>
        While working on that research, I started asking the same question of AI, which was quickly becoming part of how companies make decisions.
        If who sits around the boardroom table leaves a measurable mark on a company’s emissions, then who sits around an AI system probably matters just as much.
      </p>

      <h2 id="human-wrapped-ai">Human-wrapped AI</h2>
      <p>
        That’s the idea behind a phrase I use a lot: <strong>human-wrapped AI</strong>. AI outcomes are shaped not only by models, but by the people,
        organisations, incentives, policies, economics and governance wrapped around them. The same model can produce very different results depending on
        who decides where it is used, how it is paid for, who checks its work, and who is accountable when it is wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/videos">WRAP Framework</a>, a simple diagnostic for working out what an AI system actually is, is one early attempt to make that practical.
      </p>

      <h2>This site</h2>
      <p>
        This is where I publish research, essays, notes and the occasional experiment, all under my own name.
        Some earlier experiments I published as AI Experimenter may move here over time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="contact">
      <p>The best way to reach me is on LinkedIn.</p>
      <a class="btn btn--signal" href={SITE.linkedin} rel="me noopener" target="_blank">connect on linkedin <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
<style>
  .about-body { margin-top: 1rem; }
  .prose h2:first-child { margin-top: 0.5rem; }
  .contact { max-width: var(--measure); margin-top: 3.5rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .contact p { margin-bottom: 1.1rem; color: var(--muted); }
</style>
